1
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,197
In this video we work on the sample two-storey family house.

2
00:00:25,197 --> 00:00:29,495
To get the same house, open Demo, choose it, and click Keep this house.

3
00:00:29,495 --> 00:00:33,333
Every floor of the house has its own tab at the top of the screen.

4
00:00:33,333 --> 00:00:38,823
The plan always shows and edits one floor at a time: the one whose tab is highlighted.

5
00:00:38,823 --> 00:00:40,624
Click the Upper floor tab.

6
00:00:40,624 --> 00:00:43,842
Now we see the bedrooms and the bathroom upstairs.

7
00:00:43,842 --> 00:00:50,202
The walls of the other floors are drawn as faint dashed lines, so you can line up walls between storeys.

8
00:00:50,202 --> 00:00:54,565
The Other floors switch in the toolbar turns those dashed outlines off,

9
00:00:54,565 --> 00:00:55,788
and on again.

10
00:00:55,788 --> 00:01:08,050
The small dashed tabs between the floors, such as plus one point five and plus two point five, add a split level: a floor halfway between two storeys, for example a landing or a mezzanine.

11
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:14,480
With nothing selected, the Plan settings on the right have buttons to add a whole new upper floor or a basement.

12
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,706
Here you also set each floor's name and wall height.

13
00:01:17,706 --> 00:01:19,915
The Height bar sets the working height.

14
00:01:19,915 --> 00:01:28,776
New walls, stairs, balconies and roofs are placed at the height you choose here, so a roof, for example, can sit exactly on top of the walls.

15
00:01:28,776 --> 00:01:30,822
Let's go back to the ground floor.

16
00:01:31,267 --> 00:01:37,479
For detailed work, zoom in first: scroll the mouse wheel over the part of the plan you want to see.

17
00:01:37,479 --> 00:01:39,428
Click an object to select it.

18
00:01:39,428 --> 00:01:41,658
Here, the sofa in the living room.

19
00:01:41,658 --> 00:01:48,354
In Properties, Position X and Y place the object exactly, in metres from the top left of the plan.

20
00:01:48,354 --> 00:01:52,730
Z lifts it off the floor, for a shelf or a wall cupboard, for example.

21
00:01:52,730 --> 00:01:55,950
Below that, Size sets its length, width and height.

22
00:01:55,950 --> 00:02:00,468
The arrow keys nudge the selected object one centimetre at a time.

23
00:02:00,468 --> 00:02:03,660
Hold Shift, and each press moves it ten centimetres.

24
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:07,539
Watch the X value change as I press Shift and the right arrow.

25
00:02:07,539 --> 00:02:10,808
Press H to flip an object, like a mirror image.

26
00:02:10,808 --> 00:02:12,838
Press it again to flip it back.

27
00:02:12,838 --> 00:02:18,410
The orientation buttons in Properties turn it to any multiple of forty-five degrees.

28
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:23,338
The Dimensions button shows the measurements of the walls and rooms right on the plan.

29
00:02:23,338 --> 00:02:31,667
For your own measuring lines, use the Dimension icons in the Walls and structure category, with or without a label.

30
00:02:31,667 --> 00:02:34,672
Snap keeps objects on the grid as you drag them.

31
00:02:34,672 --> 00:02:38,064
Hold the Alt key while dragging to place something freely.

32
00:02:38,064 --> 00:02:41,216
The grid size itself is set in Plan settings.

33
00:02:41,216 --> 00:02:46,470
Align fits doors and windows neatly into walls, and joins the ends of walls together.

34
00:02:46,470 --> 00:02:50,423
Leave both switched on unless you need to place something by hand.

35
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:55,464
To select several objects at once, hold Shift and drag a box around them.

36
00:02:55,464 --> 00:02:57,625
Here, around the kitchen units.

37
00:02:57,625 --> 00:03:01,814
Shift and click adds or removes single objects from the selection.

38
00:03:01,814 --> 00:03:04,954
Control A selects everything on the current floor.

39
00:03:04,954 --> 00:03:07,864
Properties now lists everything that is selected.

40
00:03:07,864 --> 00:03:16,458
From here you can align the objects to each other, spread them out evenly, rotate them as a group, or group them, so they always move together.

41
00:03:16,458 --> 00:03:20,409
Control C copies the selection and Control V pastes it.

42
00:03:20,409 --> 00:03:30,198
A useful trick: paste on another floor, and the objects keep exactly the same position, ideal for walls that must line up between storeys.

43
00:03:30,198 --> 00:03:34,590
The Objects tab on the left lists everything in the plan, sorted by type.

44
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:35,961
You can search it.

45
00:03:35,961 --> 00:03:37,403
Let's type car,

46
00:03:37,403 --> 00:03:40,202
and click the entry to select the car on the plan.

47
00:03:40,202 --> 00:03:49,177
Each row also has buttons to hide that object in 3D, lock it in place so it can't be moved by accident, or delete it.

48
00:03:49,233 --> 00:03:52,686
Prefabs are ready-made rooms and groups of objects.

49
00:03:52,686 --> 00:03:54,310
Click the Prefab tab.

50
00:03:54,310 --> 00:04:04,524
There are garages, kitchens, dining rooms, bathrooms, living rooms, bedrooms, a home office and several balconies, each with its size and number of objects.

51
00:04:04,524 --> 00:04:08,580
Tick With walls, doors and windows to get the room's walls as well.

52
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:14,233
Untick it to place just the furniture, for example inside a room you have already drawn.

53
00:04:14,233 --> 00:04:17,439
Drag a prefab onto the plan, just like an icon.

54
00:04:17,439 --> 00:04:19,078
Here, a home office.

55
00:04:19,078 --> 00:04:25,670
A prefab is placed as a group: one click selects the whole thing, so you can move it or turn it in one go.

56
00:04:25,670 --> 00:04:32,692
To pick out a single piece, hold Alt while you click.

57
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:35,917
Let's zoom in on the front of the house, and select a wall.

58
00:04:35,917 --> 00:04:40,719
In Properties you can change the wall's thickness and height, and its material.

59
00:04:40,719 --> 00:04:46,620
First choose what to paint: the whole wall, only its outside face, or only its inside face.

60
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:50,377
That way a wall can be brick outside and plaster inside.

61
00:04:50,377 --> 00:04:55,804
Then pick a material: plaster, brick, stone, wood, concrete, tiles, siding and more.

62
00:04:55,804 --> 00:04:58,282
Let's give the outside face brick.

63
00:04:58,282 --> 00:05:05,537
Materials are shown as hatching on the plan, and as real textures in the 3D view.

64
00:05:05,537 --> 00:05:10,565
If walls don't quite meet, or a door sits slightly outside its wall, use Tidy up.

65
00:05:10,565 --> 00:05:18,657
Fit doors and windows tidies the current floor: every door and window is fitted into its wall, and gaps between wall ends are closed.

66
00:05:18,657 --> 00:05:26,811
Adjust whole house does this for every floor from the ground up, and also lines up walls, slabs and stairs between storeys.

67
00:05:26,811 --> 00:05:31,369
Fill garden, or the G key, lays lawn, gravel or paving outdoors.

68
00:05:31,369 --> 00:05:37,705
Click inside an area enclosed by walls, fences or hedges, or drag a rectangle, like this.

69
00:05:37,705 --> 00:05:41,434
Its material is chosen in Properties, just like a floor.

70
00:05:41,733 --> 00:05:43,696
Open the 3D view again.

71
00:05:43,696 --> 00:05:45,805
There are more tools in its toolbar.

72
00:05:45,805 --> 00:05:50,089
This floor shows only the floor you are designing, and the ones below it.

73
00:05:50,089 --> 00:05:53,211
Whole house shows every storey at its real height.

74
00:05:53,211 --> 00:05:59,544
Exploded pulls the floors apart, so you can look into every level at once.

75
00:05:59,544 --> 00:06:02,150
Click an object in 3D to select it.

76
00:06:02,150 --> 00:06:03,411
Here, the roof.

77
00:06:03,411 --> 00:06:05,218
Then click Hide selected.

78
00:06:05,218 --> 00:06:08,854
With the roof hidden, we can look down into the upstairs rooms.

79
00:06:08,854 --> 00:06:11,263
See-through would make it transparent instead.

80
00:06:11,263 --> 00:06:13,267
Show all brings everything back.

81
00:06:13,267 --> 00:06:19,034
Paint faces lets you click a single side of a wall in 3D and give it its own material.

82
00:06:19,034 --> 00:06:24,947
The ground menu sets the surroundings: plain, lawn, trees, a street, paving or gravel.

83
00:06:24,947 --> 00:06:26,905
Let's choose a street with trees.

84
00:06:26,905 --> 00:06:29,500
Close 3D takes us back to the plan.

85
00:06:29,933 --> 00:06:33,353
The Settings button is the small gear at the bottom left.

86
00:06:33,353 --> 00:06:38,428
Appearance switches the panels between light and dark, or follows your computer's setting.

87
00:06:38,428 --> 00:06:41,043
Units switch between metric and imperial.

88
00:06:41,043 --> 00:06:42,646
Let's try imperial.

89
00:06:42,646 --> 00:06:49,925
Now lengths are shown in feet and inches, and you can type them that way too, such as ten foot six.

90
00:06:49,925 --> 00:06:56,102
The last setting decides whether new stairs start, or end, at the height chosen in the Height bar.

91
00:06:56,102 --> 00:06:59,534
Finally, the File menu has more than Save and Open.

92
00:06:59,534 --> 00:07:03,434
3D simulator saves a stand-alone web page of your house.

93
00:07:03,434 --> 00:07:10,356
Anyone who opens it can orbit around the house, or walk through the rooms at eye height, without installing anything.

94
00:07:10,356 --> 00:07:24,372
Export for AI writes a full description of the house: every room, wall, door, window, material and measurement, together with a ready-made prompt you can give to an AI image tool to create a realistic picture.

95
00:07:24,372 --> 00:07:28,115
And remember: a house built by the Wizard is never final.

96
00:07:28,115 --> 00:07:33,467
After building, Change answers takes you back to adjust any answer and build it again.

